<!--
    Every bank on Thailand's interbank clearing list, the one the e-payment
    layouts take the receiving bank code from. The Bank of Thailand (001) and
    Thanachart (065, merged into ttb) are left out: neither holds a payee's
    account.

    Reference data, not demo data. A payee's account and the institute's own
    must point at the *same* record for their bank: auto-matching compares the
    records themselves, not their codes, so a second copy of a bank silently
    sends every payee at it to the fallback paying account.

    ``hooks._seed_bank_accounts`` matches its table against these by BIC to
    attach each paying account to its bank, and that bank is what auto-matching
    compares a payee's own bank against and what the e-payment file names as the
    sending bank.

    The BIC is the key on purpose: it is what the bank export modules themselves
    switch on (``KRTHTHBK`` for KTB, ``SICOTHBK`` for SCB, …), so a record that
    carries the right one is a record those modules already recognise. Thai
    Credit's is left blank: the directories disagree on it.

    ``bank_code`` is the bank's clearing code. The e-payment layouts write it
    straight into the file as the receiving bank — KTB's D record and SCB's 003
    record both do — so an empty one is not "rejected visibly" at all: the field
    goes out as spaces and the bank returns the whole file. The codes are
    public, fixed, and the same for every customer of each bank (taken from
    SCB's own upload manual), so there is nothing for the treasury office to
    decide.

    ``bank_branch_code`` is still left blank on purpose. It is per branch, not
    per bank, so no single value on a bank record can be right for every payee;
    SCB's layout now takes the receiving branch from the first three digits of
    the payee's own account number instead, which is where SCB puts it.

    ``short_name`` is the treasury office's own everyday abbreviation, read by
    nothing but a human — no bank file uses it.

    ``noupdate`` means an upgrade never re-applies a value changed here on a
    database where the record already exists; such a change needs a one-off
    data fix at deploy.
-->
